r1.push(P("Your next step. Walk your retail shelf today and count how many products are linked to a specific treatment result and a written regimen. If the answer is few, you have just found profit that is already inside your practice. Start with curation, then prescribe. The shelf does the rest.", { before: 160, after: 60 }));

/* ===================================================================== */
/* PLAYBOOK 2: COMPENSATION AND INCENTIVE                                 */
/* ===================================================================== */
numIdx = 0;
const c1 = [];
c1.push(...titleBlock("THE LUXORA COLLECTIVE", "The Compensation and Incentive Playbook", "Paying Your Team in a Way That Protects Your Margin", "A course module for independent med spa owners"));

c1.push(P("Labor is almost always the largest expense in a med spa, and provider pay is where profit is quietly won or lost. A compensation plan that looks generous on the surface can leave the practice with almost nothing after product cost and overhead, and most owners do not discover this until the bank account tells them. The fix is not to pay less. It is to pay in a way that ties what your team earns to what makes the practice profitable.", { after: 140 }));
c1.push(P("This module gives you the principles, the model structures by role, the incentives that actually drive profit, and the piece most playbooks skip, how to move existing staff onto a healthier structure without losing them.", { after: 80 }));
c1.push(note("This is not legal or accounting advice. Compensation is governed by federal and state employment law, and the right structure depends on your specific numbers. Confirm any plan with qualified counsel and your accountant before you implement it."));

c1.push(H2("What This Playbook Covers"));
[
  "Part One. The Principles. What healthy compensation does, the labor cost reality, and the one rule that protects every plan.",
  "Part Two. Models by Role. Structures for providers, aestheticians and technicians, front desk, patient coordinators, and managers.",
  "Part Three. Incentives That Drive Profit. What to reward, what to never reward, and how to pay it.",
  "Part Four. Transition, Implementation, and Tracking. How to change structures cleanly and run the numbers."
].forEach(t => c1.push(bullet(t)));

c1.push(H1("Part One. The Principles"));

c1.push(H2("What Healthy Compensation Does"));
c1.push(P("Good compensation does three things at once. It attracts and keeps the talent your clients trust, it motivates the behaviors that grow the practice, and it leaves room for the practice to profit after every cost is paid. A plan that does the first two but not the third is not generous. It is a slow leak.", { after: 60 }));

c1.push(H2("The Labor Cost Reality"));
c1.push(P("In most med spas, total labor, including provider pay, front desk, and management, is the single largest line on the profit and loss statement. A common healthy target is to keep total labor below roughly forty to forty five percent of revenue, though the right number depends on your model and on whether the owner also treats. Provider compensation specifically often lands in the range of twenty five to thirty five percent of the revenue that provider generates. Treat these as reference points to test your own numbers against, not as rules. The discipline is the same either way. Know your target before you design the plan.", { after: 60 }));

c1.push(H2("The Rule That Protects Every Plan"));
c1.push(P("Never pay commission on revenue without accounting for the cost of the product behind it. This single mistake quietly destroys med spa margins, and it is the most important idea in this module.", { after: 100 }));
c1.push(P("Consider an injectable service. If a provider earns thirty percent of the service revenue, and the product itself costs forty percent of that revenue, then seventy percent of the price is committed before a single dollar reaches rent, equipment, the front desk, or owner pay. On services with high product cost, a revenue based commission can leave the practice losing money on its busiest days.", { after: 100 }));
c1.push(P("There are two clean ways to hold the line. Pay commission on a basis that accounts for product cost, for example on service revenue after the product cost is removed, or set a lower commission rate on high product cost services than on low product cost ones. Either way, model every structure against the full economics of the service before you offer it.", { after: 60 }));

c1.push(H1("Part Two. Models by Role"));
c1.push(P("There is no single correct structure. There are structures that fit a role and protect your margin, and structures that do not. Below are the common models for each seat, with the guardrails that keep them profitable.", { after: 80 }));

c1.push(H2("Providers and Injectors"));
c1.push(P("This is the seat that moves your margin the most, so design it with the most care. The common structures are below.", { after: 100 }));
[
  "Base plus commission. An hourly or salary base for stability, plus a commission on the service revenue generated. The most common structure, and usually the most balanced.",
  "Commission only, or a draw against commission. Higher upside for the provider and higher variability. Better suited to an established provider with a full book.",
  "Per treatment or per unit pay. Pay tied to units or treatments delivered. Simple, but only safe when the rate is set against the full cost of the service.",
  "Tiered commission. The commission rate rises as production passes set thresholds, which rewards growth without overpaying a light book."
].forEach(t => c1.push(bullet(t)));
c1.push(H3("Guardrails"));
[
  "Apply the product cost rule to every commission, so margin survives the busiest days.",
  "Set a production threshold before commission begins, so the base pay is earned before variable pay is added.",
  "Confirm that base plus commission still nets the practice a profit at realistic production, not only at peak.",
  "Ramp a new provider differently from an established one, with a higher base and a path to commission as the book fills."
].forEach(t => c1.push(bullet(t)));

c1.push(H2("Aestheticians and Laser Technicians"));
c1.push(P("These roles often deliver services with lower product cost than injectables, which leaves more room in the commission. A common structure is an hourly base plus a service commission, with a separate retail commission to drive homecare. Tie a share of their variable pay to retail and rebooking, the behaviors that compound over time.", { after: 60 }));

c1.push(H2("Front Desk and Coordinators"));
c1.push(P("This seat is usually hourly, and that is appropriate. Rather than pushing individual sales pressure onto the front desk, reward the team metrics they influence, such as rebooking rate, confirmed appointments, review volume, and a low no show rate. A small bonus for membership enrollments they personally drive can work, but keep the front of house focused on experience, not on closing.", { after: 60 }));

c1.push(H2("Patient Coordinators and Sales Roles"));
c1.push(P("A practice with a dedicated coordinator who handles consultations and closes treatment plans can use a commission on closed plan revenue, paid promptly, with protections in writing. Keep the structure simple, and tie it to booked and completed revenue rather than to quotes. If you use this seat, document it with the same clawback and non solicitation protections you would apply to any commissioned closer.", { after: 60 }));

c1.push(H2("Managers"));
c1.push(P("A manager is usually salaried, with a bonus tied to the outcomes a manager controls. The cleanest bonus is tied to practice profit, or to a small basket of metrics such as labor cost ratio, client retention, and a revenue target. Avoid tying a manager bonus to revenue alone, which can encourage discounting and overstaffing.", { after: 60 }));

c1.push(H1("Part Three. Incentives That Drive Profit"));

c1.push(H2("Reward the Profit Drivers, Not Just Revenue"));
c1.push(P("The fastest way to waste an incentive budget is to pay for raw revenue, which rewards discounting and overselling. Pay instead for the behaviors that build a profitable, durable practice.", { after: 100 }));
c1.push(H3("Reward these"));
[
  "Rebooking rate, which fills tomorrow's schedule.",
  "Membership enrollment, which turns one visit into recurring revenue.",
  "Retail attachment, your highest margin stream.",
  "Average ticket built through regimens and treatment plans, not through discounts.",
  "Client retention and review generation, which lower the cost of future growth."
].forEach(t => c1.push(bullet(t)));
c1.push(H3("Be cautious about these"));
[
  "Raw revenue alone, which invites discounting and pressure.",
  "Hours worked, which pays for presence rather than results.",
  "Anything that pits team members against each other in a way that harms the client experience."
].forEach(t => c1.push(bullet(t)));

c1.push(H2("A Note on Retail and Membership Spiffs"));
c1.push(P("A simple, prompt incentive on retail and on new memberships moves behavior more than a complex annual bonus does. A flat amount per regimen sold or per new member, paid visibly and soon after, is easy to understand and easy to feel. Keep it small enough to protect margin and frequent enough to stay motivating. This connects directly to your Retail as a Profit Center module.", { after: 60 }));

c1.push(H2("The Team Bonus Pool"));
c1.push(P("A monthly pool tied to a practice wide target, shared by the team, builds culture and reduces the internal competition that individual commissions can create. Use it alongside individual structures rather than instead of them, so the individual and the whole are pulling in the same direction.", { after: 60 }));

c1.push(H2("How to Pay It"));
[
  "Keep the formula simple enough to calculate without a spreadsheet.",
  "Pay on a predictable, frequent cadence, so the reward connects to the behavior that earned it.",
  "Show a visible scoreboard, so the team can see where they stand.",
  "Set the rules in writing, and do not change them in the middle of a period."
].forEach(t => c1.push(bullet(t)));

c1.push(H1("Part Four. Transition, Implementation, and Tracking"));

c1.push(H2("How to Move Existing Staff to a Healthier Structure"));
c1.push(P("Changing the pay of someone who is already producing is the most delicate move in this module. Done poorly, you lose your best provider. Done well, the provider earns the same or more and the practice keeps more too. Follow this method.", { after: 100 }));
{
  const r = nextNum();
  [
    "Model the new structure against the person's actual recent production, so you and they can see the real outcome rather than a guess.",
    "Design the new plan so a strong performer can earn the same or more by doing the things you want more of.",
    "Offer a transition period, and consider grandfathering part of the old structure while the new one phases in.",
    "Frame the change around upside and the security that comes from a practice that is actually profitable, not around taking something away.",
    "Be transparent about the math, including why product cost and margin protect everyone's job.",
    "Put the new agreement in writing, with the protections appropriate to the role, and confirm it with counsel."
  ].forEach(t => c1.push(numbered(t, r)));
}

c1.push(H2("The Implementation Roadmap"));
c1.push(new Table({ width: { size: 9360, type: WidthType.DXA }, columnWidths: [1700, 2200, 5460],
  rows: [
    ["Phase", "Focus", "What to do"],
    ["Phase 1", "Audit", "Calculate current total labor as a share of revenue, and lay each provider's earnings next to the revenue they generate."],
    ["Phase 2", "Model", "Build the economics of each service and each role, applying the product cost rule to every commission."],
    ["Phase 3", "Design", "Choose the structure for each seat, and set thresholds, rates, and incentives that leave room for profit."],
    ["Phase 4", "Communicate and transition", "Present the plan, model it against real production, and phase it in with agreements in writing."]
  ].map((r, i) => new TableRow({ children: r.map((txt, c) => {
    const widths = [1700, 2200, 5460];
    return tcell(txt, { w: widths[c], head: i === 0, bold: i !== 0 && c === 0, size: i === 0 ? 20 : 19, alt: i !== 0 && i % 2 === 0 });
  }) })) }));

c1.push(H2("The Labor Scorecard"));
c1.push(P("Maintain one monthly scorecard so labor stays a managed number. Fill in your targets, then track the actuals each month.", { after: 120 }));
c1.push(simpleTable([
  ["Metric", "Reference target", "This month", "Owner"],
  ["Total labor as percent of revenue", "Under roughly 40 to 45 percent", "", ""],
  ["Provider comp as percent of provider revenue", "Roughly 25 to 35 percent", "", ""],
  ["Front desk and admin labor as percent of revenue", "Set your own target", "", ""],
  ["Average revenue per provider", "Compare and coach", "", ""],
  ["Incentive payout as percent of revenue", "Keep deliberate and modeled", "", ""]
], [3460, 2700, 1600, 1600]));
c1.push(P("Your next step. Before you change anyone's pay, run the Phase 1 audit. Calculate your total labor as a share of revenue, and lay each provider's earnings next to the revenue they generate. The structure you need will become clear once the numbers are in front of you. Pay for profit, not just production, and put every plan in writing.", { before: 160, after: 60 }));

/* ---------- build both ---------- */
Promise.all([
  buildDoc("Retail as a Profit Center", r1, "/home/claude/Retail_as_a_Profit_Center.docx"),
  buildDoc("The Compensation and Incentive Playbook", c1, "/home/claude/Compensation_and_Incentive_Playbook.docx")
]).then(() => console.log("both written"));

